# AI-Assisted codeing

Ain't no fucking "vibe" if you are a proffesional.

Look, almost everyone in the business got their start modifying code / copying features.
It is like learning any other profession, you take things apart, mod them, put them together and figure out how they work.

Stack Overflow was a meme for a few years, because there wasn't a coder out there who in desperation haden't stumbled in there, searching for answers.



# Github Copilot, the agent that launced a thousand script kiddies.

I have already posted about what the world looked like back when I started, but one thing i didn't really get to rant about was just how much tweaking and testing AI-Code requires.
Now IT FEELS like you are making more progress when you get to the testing phase quicker, but you sure end up itterating a lot, especially when you are building something on the cutting edge.

For me getting the latest suite of Pen-Test tools working has been about 40% llm and 50% good old fashion documentation reading. 

If you want a website from 6 years ago, a script for an old stable system, or mockups then an LLM can work as a cheat sheet, throwing up a bunch of working (but not necessarily optimal) code that get's you working in the general direction you want to go.

